import{_ as a,c as t,o as i,ag as r}from"./chunks/framework.ARG558ZT.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Relational Databases, PostgreSQL, and MikroORM — practice","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"practice/node-js/module-08-postgresql-mikroorm.md","filePath":"practice/node-js/module-08-postgresql-mikroorm.md"}'),n={name:"practice/node-js/module-08-postgresql-mikroorm.md"};function o(s,e,l,d,c,p){return i(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="relational-databases-postgresql-and-mikroorm-—-practice" tabindex="-1">Relational Databases, PostgreSQL, and MikroORM — practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#relational-databases-postgresql-and-mikroorm-—-practice" aria-label="Permalink to “Relational Databases, PostgreSQL, and MikroORM — practice”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Learner status: still required.</strong> These tasks were separated from the textbook; no completion is credited.</p><p><a href="./../../node-js/module-08-postgresql-mikroorm.html">Textbook explanation</a> · <a href="./">Node.js practice map</a></p><h4 id="guided-practice" tabindex="-1">Guided practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#guided-practice" aria-label="Permalink to “Guided practice”">​</a></h4><p>In a disposable database with representative orders, run <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code> for the latest-pending-orders query before and after adding the index. Run <code>ANALYZE orders</code> after loading fixture data. Compare the scan, any separate sort, buffers and execution time. Small fixtures may correctly use a sequential scan.</p><p>Then remove the status equality and predict why sorting may reappear. <strong>Success:</strong> explain the observed plan and propose an index for the changed query, including its write/storage trade-off. Do not force index usage just to make the exercise appear successful.</p><h3 id="diagnostic-exercise" tabindex="-1">Diagnostic exercise <a class="header-anchor" href="#diagnostic-exercise" aria-label="Permalink to “Diagnostic exercise”">​</a></h3><p>Implement a money-transfer use case.</p><p>Requirements:</p><ul><li>total balance remains constant;</li><li>accounts are locked in a consistent order;</li><li>deadlock/serialization retry is bounded;</li><li>an idempotency key prevents duplicate transfers;</li><li>an outbox record is committed with the transfer;</li><li>pool and query time are measured separately;</li><li>a schema change remains compatible during rolling deployment.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="graduated-practice-persistence-and-migrations" tabindex="-1">Graduated practice: persistence and migrations <a class="header-anchor" href="#graduated-practice-persistence-and-migrations" aria-label="Permalink to “Graduated practice: persistence and migrations”">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-worked-example" tabindex="-1">1. Worked example <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-worked-example" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Worked example”">​</a></h3><p>Run the temporary-table lesson and the Workbench fixture. Compare the returned rows with their expected output before introducing an ORM.</p><h3 id="_2-guided-build" tabindex="-1">2. Guided build <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-guided-build" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Guided build”">​</a></h3><p>In the retained source under <code>examples/node-textbook</code>, inspect <code>Account</code>/<code>Task</code>, the migration, and the authenticated task routes&#39; database operations. Authentication becomes your learning objective in Module 9; the completed project already contains those routes so it remains runnable throughout.</p><h3 id="_3-independent-task" tabindex="-1">3. Independent task <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-independent-task" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Independent task”">​</a></h3><p>Add an optional due date to tasks using a new numbered migration and update the input/output schema. Hint: choose a calendar <code>date</code> if the user means a day, and distinguish omitted from explicit null. <strong>Success:</strong> a fresh database and an upgraded database both work; another user&#39;s tasks remain inaccessible; restarting the process preserves dates.</p><h3 id="_4-optional-depth-metadata-timestamp-and-seed-failures" tabindex="-1">4. Optional depth: metadata, timestamp and seed failures <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-optional-depth-metadata-timestamp-and-seed-failures" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Optional depth: metadata, timestamp and seed failures”">​</a></h3><p>In the separate User modeling exercise, create the exact primary project files shown above: package/tsconfig, src/mikro-orm.config.ts, src/entities/user.entity.ts and src/seed.ts. Supply the authorized database configuration, run a locked install/typecheck/build, and verify metadata discovery/Migrator registration on the compiled path. Generate/review/compile/apply a migration against a disposable database. Run source and compiled entity discovery intentionally; do not require both source and built entities in one process. Relationship snippets are extensions needing their complete reciprocal fields/entities, not a second already-complete program.</p><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> the compiled User boots with reflection and the explicit legacy provider; migration CLI loads its compiled config and emits source to the requested path; missing configuration fails before database work; two sequential and two concurrent reference seeds yield one unique row and preserve an existing value; a failed seed still closes; the process terminates without a forced success exit. Pin/retest the exact ORM version rather than extrapolate from v6-style imports. Standard decorators/defineEntity remain alternative paths.</p><p>For the products SQL, insert a row, update it in a later statement and prove DEFAULT NOW() alone did not maintain updated_at. Apply the trigger and repeat; confirm statement-time behavior and explain ORM onUpdate versus external SQL. Use a disposable schema and clean it up. This demonstrates one selected timestamp policy, not every timezone/DST behavior (covered in a separate date lesson).</p><p>References checked 2026-10-21: <a href="https://mikro-orm.io/docs/upgrading-v6-to-v7" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MikroORM 7 migration</a>, <a href="https://mikro-orm.io/docs/metadata-providers#reflectmetadataprovider" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">legacy reflection provider</a>, <a href="https://mikro-orm.io/docs/migrations" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">migration configuration</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-datetime.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PostgreSQL date/time functions</a>, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/plpgsql-trigger.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">triggers</a>. Keep a short record of the commands, observed timestamps and seed row counts. Hosted migration and recovery drills are separate exercises.</p><p>For the executable SQL-only companion, use the <a href="./../../workbench/node-js/database/sql-queries.html">SQL Query Workbench</a>. It covers parameterized CRUD, grouping/NULL/HAVING, subqueries, CTEs and window frames with one disposable fixture and explicit expected results.</p><hr><h2 id="_24-final-review-checklist" tabindex="-1">24. Final review checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#_24-final-review-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to “24. Final review checklist”">​</a></h2><h3 id="concurrency-and-operations" tabindex="-1">Concurrency and operations <a class="header-anchor" href="#concurrency-and-operations" aria-label="Permalink to “Concurrency and operations”">​</a></h3><ul><li>[ ] Choose optimistic or pessimistic locking from expected conflict behavior.</li><li>[ ] Explain how deadlocks occur and why PostgreSQL aborts one participant.</li><li>[ ] Retry only a complete, repeatable transaction with bounded backoff.</li><li>[ ] Keep irreversible external effects outside retryable callbacks.</li><li>[ ] Size pools across all replicas and reserve operational capacity.</li><li>[ ] Separate pool waiting, query execution, and lock waiting during diagnosis.</li><li>[ ] Use expand-and-contract migrations for rolling deployment compatibility.</li></ul><h3 id="relational-foundations" tabindex="-1">Relational foundations <a class="header-anchor" href="#relational-foundations" aria-label="Permalink to “Relational foundations”">​</a></h3><ul><li>[ ] Explain tables, rows, columns, types, keys, and constraints.</li><li>[ ] Describe PostgreSQL server → database → schema → table hierarchy.</li><li>[ ] Choose UUID or identity keys from real trade-offs.</li><li>[ ] Model one-to-one, one-to-many, and many-to-many relationships.</li><li>[ ] Select deliberate foreign-key delete behavior.</li></ul><h3 id="modeling-and-sql" tabindex="-1">Modeling and SQL <a class="header-anchor" href="#modeling-and-sql" aria-label="Permalink to “Modeling and SQL”">​</a></h3><ul><li>[ ] Identify insertion, update, and deletion anomalies.</li><li>[ ] Apply 1NF, 2NF, and 3NF from functional dependencies.</li><li>[ ] Use inner, left, right, full outer, multi-table, and self-joins.</li><li>[ ] Explain when <code>NULL</code> appears in outer-join results.</li><li>[ ] Denormalize only for measured performance or historical semantics.</li></ul><h3 id="performance-and-transactions" tabindex="-1">Performance and transactions <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance-and-transactions" aria-label="Permalink to “Performance and transactions”">​</a></h3><ul><li>[ ] Design single-column, composite, and partial indexes from queries.</li><li>[ ] Explain composite-index column order.</li><li>[ ] Read essential <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> output.</li><li>[ ] Define Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability.</li><li>[ ] Use atomic conditional updates, locks, or Serializable isolation for races.</li><li>[ ] Keep transactions short and handle retryable failures safely.</li></ul><h3 id="mikroorm" tabindex="-1">MikroORM <a class="header-anchor" href="#mikroorm" aria-label="Permalink to “MikroORM”">​</a></h3><ul><li>[ ] Match decorator imports/configuration to the installed major version.</li><li>[ ] Configure PostgreSQL, entities, migrations, and lifecycle cleanup.</li><li>[ ] Explain EntityManager, repository, Identity Map, and Unit of Work.</li><li>[ ] Implement CRUD with a request-scoped EntityManager.</li><li>[ ] Model owning/inverse sides and <code>Collection&lt;T&gt;</code> relations.</li><li>[ ] Use population deliberately and avoid N+1 queries.</li><li>[ ] Generate, review, apply, and test migrations.</li><li>[ ] Use implicit flush transactions and explicit <code>transactional()</code> scopes.</li></ul><p><strong>Guided practice:</strong> recreate the table, add a third unfinished task, and return only unfinished titles in ID order. Then count unfinished tasks. Hint: filter before grouping/counting. <strong>Success:</strong> your list contains exactly the unfinished rows and the count agrees with the list length.</p><p><strong>Worked failure:</strong> use an unreachable port in your disposable database configuration. Startup must reject before HTTP begins listening and the process must terminate with a nonzero status. Restore the correct port and verify <code>SELECT 1</code> succeeds. The retained runnable application implements this ownership contract in <code>src/database.ts</code>.</p>',38)])])}const m=a(n,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
